<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sabzevar</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sabzawar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sabzevar is a city in Razavi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a> province in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nishapur">Nishapur</a> on the old road between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mashhad">Mashhad</a>. In the 14th century it was the capital of the Sarbadars, a Shia state formed in a revolt against Mongol officials after the break-up of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a>. It was long a centre of Shia learning and is the birthplace of the 19th-century philosopher Hadi Sabzavari.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sabzevar.jpg" alt="Sabzevar" width="320" height="211" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hadidehghanpour · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mulla_Hadi_Sabzevari_Tomb_(ADI3475-05).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Sabzevar is a city in northeastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, in the Central District of Sabzevar County in Razavi Khorasan province, and serves as the capital of both the county and the district. It lies in the west of the province, approximately 220 kilometres west of the provincial capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mashhad">Mashhad</a> and 650 km from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a>. Nishapur, Bardaskan, Esfarāyen and Shahrud are notable neighbouring settlements. The climate is cool semi-arid, with sweltering and virtually rainless summers and cool winters with occasional, generally light, showers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The history of Sabzevar goes back to the 1st millennium BCE. Ancient remains include the fire-temple Azarbarzin, which is still visible. After the Mongol invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, the city was the first part of Iran to move towards its freedom, under the lead of the Sarbedaran movement.</p><p>The province of Sabzevar had been lost by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbeks</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a>, but it was regained after a Safavid counter-offensive around 1600, together with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> and Farah. In 1937, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> was ruled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reza-shah">Reza Shah</a>, Sabzevar was the second most populated city in the ninth county. Hakim Sabzevari University was established in 1973 as Kar University, suspended after the revolution of 1979 until 1987, and re-established under a new name.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Sabzevar is the commercial centre for an agricultural region producing grapes and raisins. There is some small-scale industry, including food processing, copperware and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/electric-motor">electric motors</a>, and fresh, dried and preserved fruits and vegetables are exported through the old bazaar. The city is connected to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mashhad">Mashhad</a> by road and has an airport, opened in 2003. Its population was 243,700 at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 2016, living in 74,757 households.</p><p>Among the sights are Mil-e Khosrow Gerd, a brick tower named for King Khosrau and the tallest brick tower in the city, and the Friday mosque, Masjed Jameh, with two tall minarets, both built in the Islamic age of Sabzevar. Notable people connected with the city include Hadi Sabzavari, Ali Shariati, the writer Mahmoud Dowlatabadi, the historian Abul-Fazl Bayhaqi and Husayn Kashifi.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sabzevar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the 1330s, as the Ilkhanate disintegrated, villagers at Bashtin near <b>Sabzevar</b> rose against abusive Mongol officials.</blockquote><a href="/state/sarbadars">Sarbadars</a></li></ul></section>
